\label{th:Frobenius} {\rm (Informal formulation)} Let $X$ be as in Theorem~{\rm \ref{cons:psl2B}}. Then there is a Monte-Carlo algorithm which constructs a map
 \begin{diagram}
 X & \rOnto^\phi & X
 \end{diagram}
that corresponds to the Frobenius automorphism $a \mapsto a^p$ of the field $\mathbb{F}_q$ and leaves invariant subgroups $U$ and\/ $T$ and the elements\/ $u$ and\/ $w$ of\/ $X$.

 The running time of the algorithm is quadratic in $p$ and polynomial in   $\log q$.

It suffices to observe that the action of the canonical Frobenius map
\[
F: \begin{bmatrix} a_{11}& a_{12}\\ a_{21}&a_{22}\end{bmatrix} \mapsto \begin{bmatrix} a_{11}^p& a_{12}^p\\ a_{21}^p&a_{22}^p\end{bmatrix}
\]
on the preimages of $\bar{u}, \bar{w}, \bar{h}$ in ${\rm{PSL}}_2(q)$ and their images under the powers of the Frobenius map looks like that:
\begin{eqnarray*}
\begin{bmatrix} 1&1\\ 0&1\end{bmatrix}^{F^i} &=& \begin{bmatrix} 1&1\\ 0&1\end{bmatrix},\\ \begin{bmatrix} 0&1\\ -1&0\end{bmatrix}^{F^i} &=& \begin{bmatrix} 0&1\\ -1&0\end{bmatrix},\\ \begin{bmatrix} t^{p^l}&0\\ 0&t^{p^{-l}}\end{bmatrix}^{F^i} &=& \begin{bmatrix} t^{p^{l+i \pmod k}}&0\\ 0&t^{p^{-l-i \pmod k}}\end{bmatrix}\\ &=& \begin{bmatrix} t^{p^l}&0\\ 0&t^{p^{-1}}\end{bmatrix}^{p^i}.
\end{eqnarray*}
Therefore the black box group $Y$ is generated in the direct product $X^k$ by elements
\begin{eqnarray*}
\bar{u} &=& (u,u,\dots,u)\\
\bar{w} &=& (w,w,\dots, w)\\
\bar{h} &=& (h,h^p,\dots, h^{p^{k-1}})
\end{eqnarray*}
fits precisely in the construction described in Theorem~\ref{th:automorphism}.

It remains to notice that, by nature of its construction, the map $\alpha$ in Theorem~\ref{th:automorphism} leaves invariant elements $\bar{u}$, $\bar{w}$ and the torus $\bar T$ generated by $\bar{h}$ and hence leaves invariant the unipotent group $\bar{U} = \langle \bar{u}^{\bar{T}}\rangle $ and the Borel subgroup $\bar{U}\bar{T}$ of $Y$.

Let $X$ be a black box group  encrypting ${\rm{SL}}_2(2^n)$ for some \textup{(}perhaps unknown\textup{)} $n$. We assume that we  are given an involution $u \in X$.

Then there exists a Monte-Carlo algorithm which constructs, in  polynomial in $l(X)$ time,
 \begin{itemize}
 \item a black box field\/ $\mathbb{U}$ encrypting\/ $\mathbb{F}_{2^n}$,  and
 \item  a  polynomial in $l(X)$ time isomorphism
\[\Phi: {\rm{SL}}_2(\mathbb{U}) \longrightarrow X.\]
\end{itemize}
\label{th:sl(2,2n)}

\label{cons:psl2}
Let $X$ be a black box group encrypting the group $G\cong  ( {\rm{P}} ) {\rm{SL}}_2(q)$, where $q \equiv 1  \bmod 4$ and $q=p^k$ for some $k\geqslant 1$ {\rm(}perhaps unknown{\rm)}. If $p\neq 5,7$, then there is a Monte-Carlo algorithm which constructs,  in time quadratic in $p$ and polynomial in $\log q$,
\begin{itemize}
\item  a black box field\/ $\mathbb{K}$ encrypting\/ $\mathbb{F}_{q}$,  and
\item a quadratic in $p$ and polynomial in $\log q$ time isomorphism
\[
\Phi: (P)SL_2(\mathbb{K}) \longrightarrow X.
\]
\end{itemize}
If $p=5$ or $7$, and $k$ has a small divisor $\ell$, the same result holds where the running time is polynomial in $\log q$ and quadratic in $p^\ell$.
